\description{
Construct or attach a union-formation specification without executing it. In
on-demand mode, at a birth use the woman's current partner, or choose a
partner from all living, unpartnered men using conditional-logit
probabilities from the matching formula to form a union. Men below
\code{min_male_age} are excluded. If no eligible man exists, cancel the birth by
default; \code{cancel_birth_if_no_match = FALSE} preserves it with unknown father.
Known kin within \code{kinship_generations} on both sides of a common ancestor are
excluded. Each person may have only one ongoing union. Partnered men are not
available; surviving husbands return to the pool at widowhood. Death ends
affected unions. In rate mode unpartnered women face competing search events
and use their unpartnered fertility schedule and may give birth without
initiating a search. Failed searches leave the woman searching; formation and
widowhood switch fertility schedules while preserving spacing. An overall
fertility schedule applies to both statuses, with a simulation warning.
On-demand mode requires an overall fertility schedule. Unpartnered rate-mode
births use the same eligibility and exact draw to assign a father, using
\code{father_matching} when supplied or otherwise \code{matching}, without forming a
union or removing him from availability. A search with no eligible man
cancels the birth by default. No previous-father preference is applied.
}
\details{
Partners are drawn exactly from the full conditional-logit distribution over
all eligible men, without a candidate sample. Living, unpartnered men are
grouped by group and quarter-year birth bin; a bin is chosen in proportion to
its size times the largest weight any member could have, a member is accepted
with the ratio of his weight to that maximum, and only he is screened for age
and kinship. For the built-in linear/quadratic age model this is exact.
Custom R formulas are bounded on a 1/32-year grid; if a drawn man's utility
exceeds the bound, simulation warns that draws were approximate. Existing
unions are retained even if a new-search restriction would exclude the
partner.

Matching always uses calibrated male-group weights: a woman chooses among
screened candidates with each man's selection weight multiplied by a weight
for his group. Weights are recalculated at every search, in continuous time,
so that expected male union flows by group match a mirrored target: each
unpartnered man has the per-capita intensity of an unpartnered woman of his
group, adjusted by the matching model's \code{delta}. Women's intensity is the
search hazard for rate-mode searches and the unpartnered fertility hazard
(zero during birth spacing) for on-demand searches and nonmarital father
selection, which has its own weights and uses the father model's preferences
and delta when one is supplied. Weights depend only on the man's group, so
they do not change the association between partners' characteristics in the
static group-only illustration. With one group, all group weights are one.
When no exact weights exist, approximate weights are used with a warning.

\code{union_formation()} creates the process specification;
\code{set_union_formation()} creates it and attaches it to a model through
\code{\link[=set_process]{set_process()}}. Neither function runs a simulation.
}
\section{Calendar rates}{

Search \code{rates} and \code{union_convert_rate} optionally accept \code{time_start} and
\code{time_end} for half-open absolute calendar-year bands, including fractional
years. Both columns are required together, with finite starts and greater
ends (possibly Inf). Every active slice must satisfy its existing rate rules
and cover the requested run. Clock choices stay consistent across periods;
changes precede same-time events and retain duration origins. \code{cohab_prob}
accepts the same calendar columns, with probabilities selected at the search
instant. Probability-only changes refresh matching calibration without
changing event clocks. All three matching arguments also accept whole-model
schedule tibbles with \code{time_start}, \code{time_end}, and list-column \code{model}.
Model changes update role-specific preferences and calibration before
same-time choices without changing event clocks. All supplied models,
including future intervals, are validated; gaps in a run are errors. NULL
father/cohabitation matching dynamically follows general matching. Other
non-rate arguments remain static.
